 "post": {
  "title": "Say a Word Until It Breaks #shorts",
  "description": "Repeat a word over and over and it stops meaning anything. In 1907 psychologists Elizabeth Severance and Margaret Floy Washburn had people stare at a single word for three minutes; the words lost their meaning. Leon Jakobovits James named it semantic satiation in 1962. One explanation is that the brain's response to a word's meaning tires with repetition, then recovers when you stop. #psychology #shorts #brain #language",
  "tags": [
   "semantic satiation",
   "language",
   "brain",
   "psychology",
   "weird brain facts"
  ]
